---
/**
 * Protocol — the slice lifecycle (pending → coded → reviewed → done, with the
 * blocked → rework return path) as an inline diagram with a staged,
 * scroll-triggered reveal.
 *
 * This component used to carry the site's one permitted script (ADR-0002).
 * ADR-0005 generalised that script into a shared observer owned by
 * index.astro: the section now opts in with `data-reveal` like every other
 * section, and receives the same `is-revealed` class from the page. The
 * staged CSS below is unchanged — it was already keyed off that class, which
 * is what made the generalisation a deletion rather than a rewrite. What was
 * lost with the local script is nothing: it observed this same section
 * element, at the same default threshold, and unobserved on first hit.
 *
 * Copy is rendered verbatim from docs/copy.md §Protocol (ADR-0003: the copy
 * deck is the single source of truth). Every copy string sits on ONE source
 * line and is never split by an inline element, so the built output greps
 * literally against the deck (convention from phase-1-ws-1.1, documented in
 * phase-1-ws-1.2). Two consequences worth naming:
 *
 *   - The deck's captions already begin with the state name ("pending — …"),
 *     so the states carry no separate heading element: one would duplicate the
 *     word, and wrapping the name in a <span> to style it would put markup
 *     inside the string. The state name loses distinct typography; a literal
 *     cross-vendor grep is worth more than that.
 *   - `metis next` is backticked in the deck but renders as plain text here,
 *     for the same reason — a <code> element inside the caption would break
 *     the grep.
 *
 * Structure is the diagram: <ol> carries the order, so the flow reads
 * correctly with CSS disabled and the connectors between states can be purely
 * decorative CSS. It carries an explicit role="list" because `list-style:
 * none` makes WebKit drop the list role, and the order that role conveys is
 * the whole reason the connectors are allowed to be decorative (f-005).
 * The "blocked → rework" arrow is different — that string IS rendered copy,
 * so its "→" is a real character in the markup rather than a CSS ::before,
 * exactly as phase-1-ws-1.2 treats its em dashes.
 *
 * The return path (f-005). The block path leaves `reviewed` and re-enters
 * `coded`, and the diagram has to say so in both channels:
 *
 *   - Semantically, the label lives inside the `reviewed` <li>, so the
 *     CSS-disabled and screen-reader order is "reviewed … / blocked → rework"
 *     before `done`, not stranded after the end of the flow.
 *   - Visually, a dashed rail runs from `reviewed` back to `coded` and
 *     terminates in an arrowhead on `coded`'s own edge, so the path has a
 *     direction and two real endpoints.
 *
 * The <span> around the label is a positioning hook, not a split: the copy
 * string is the span's entire contents, one uninterrupted text node, so the
 * dist grep still matches it whole (the constraint in note 2 above is about
 * markup *inside* a string). Same for the caption the <p> now follows.
 *
 * Three stacked-layout changes fall out of the rail's geometry rather than
 * from taste — CSS cannot resolve the endpoints without them:
 *
 *   - `grid-auto-rows: 1fr` equalises the rows, which is what makes "half a
 *     card up from `reviewed`'s middle" (`calc(-50% - …)`) reach `coded`'s
 *     middle; with content-sized rows that distance is unknowable in CSS.
 *   - the row gap widens to --space-6 so the rail's label band sits between
 *     `coded` and `reviewed` without overlapping either card.
 *   - the list gains a --space-5 inline-start padding to host the rail's
 *     vertical run, so it never overflows the section.
 *
 * Color: aegean is decorative-only in here. #2E8C83 on the card surface
 * #171B22 is 4.27:1 — below AA for text, and a pairing tokens.css's ratio
 * table does not cover (it documents aegean on the base #0E1116 at 4.68:1).
 * So aegean draws connectors and the loop chip's boundary, which carry no
 * text and no information the markup lacks; all diagram text uses
 * --color-text / --color-muted, whose pairings are in that table.
 *
 * Animation (ADR-0002): the base rules render the finished diagram. The
 * staged reveal lives entirely inside `prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference`
 * and is keyed off an `is-revealed` class, with `animation-fill-mode:
 * backwards` holding each state's start frame through its stagger delay —
 * that is what avoids an `opacity: 0` base. With JS off, the observer
 * unsupported, or reduced motion set, the diagram is complete and static,
 * never hidden (ADR-0005's never-invisible rule).
 *
 * `data-reveal` is on the section rather than on the diagram, and the class
 * therefore lands on the section too — which is what keeps the scoped
 * selectors below matching, since the section is declared here. Watching the
 * section is also the only correct choice: intersectionRatio is a fraction of
 * the target's own height, so a fractional threshold on a diagram taller than
 * the viewport could never be met on a narrow phone and the reveal would
 * never fire. index.astro's observer sets no threshold for exactly that
 * reason.
 *
 * The h2 and the closing line take the page's generic `.reveal` treatment;
 * the diagram itself does not, because the staged rules below already are its
 * reveal. Putting `.reveal` on the <ol> as well would fade the whole diagram
 * in on top of its own staging.
 */
---

<section id="protocol" class="section" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="reveal">One slice at a time. Every time.</h2>

  <ol class="lifecycle" role="list">
    <li class="state">pending — dispatched by metis next, never chosen by the agent</li>
    <li class="state">coded — brief committed first, verify green, scope audited</li>
    <li class="state">reviewed — a different agent signs off, identity checked<p class="loop"><span class="loop-label">blocked → rework</span></p></li>
    <li class="state">done — archived with the full paper trail</li>
  </ol>

  <p class="closing reveal">Findings become rules. Rules prevent repeats. The system gets better because it remembers.</p>
</section>

<style>
  .section {
    padding-block: var(--space-8);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  /* --- The flow ----------------------------------------------------------
     Mobile-first: a single column, chained top to bottom. The four-across
     row is one media query at 1024px — below that the captions would be
     ~28 characters wide and the "diagram" would read as four tall slivers.
     A two-column intermediate step is deliberately skipped: it would wrap
     the chain mid-flow and the connectors would have to change direction
     between items 2 and 3. */
  .lifecycle {
    display: grid;
    /* Equal rows: the return rail measures back to coded's middle as
       "half a card plus the gap", which only holds if every card is the
       same height (f-005). */
    grid-auto-rows: 1fr;
    /* Wide enough to host the rail's label band between two cards. */
    gap: var(--space-6);
    margin-block-start: var(--space-6);
    /* Drops the UA list indent — the token zero, not a raw 0 (f-003) — then
       reopens an inline-start gutter for the rail's vertical run, so it has
       somewhere to go that is not on top of the cards or outside the
       section. */
    padding: var(--space-0);
    padding-inline-start: var(--space-5);
    list-style: none;
  }

  /* Text on surface: 16.14:1 dark, 18.91:1 light (tokens.css ratio table).
     The hairline only sharpens an edge the fill already draws, so the
     decorative --color-border is the right token, as in Personas. */
  .state {
    position: relative;
    padding: var(--space-4);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background-color: var(--color-surface);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  /* --- Connectors --------------------------------------------------------
     Decorative: the <ol> already carries order and direction, so nothing is
     lost when these are unsupported, unstyled, or unseen. ::before is the
     line, ::after the head; the line's length matches the grid gap exactly,
     so it spans the space between two cards and touches neither. */
  .state:not(:first-child)::before,
  .state:not(:first-child)::after {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    background-color: var(--color-secondary);
  }

  .state:not(:first-child)::before {
    inset-block-end: 100%;
    inset-inline-start: 50%;
    width: 2px;
    height: var(--space-6);
    transform: translateX(-50%);
  }

  /* Two borders on a rotated square make the chevron: 135deg points it down
     the column. translate(-50%, 50%) centres it on the card's top edge, at
     the end of the line above. */
  .state:not(:first-child)::after {
    inset-block-end: 100%;
    inset-inline-start: 50%;
    width: var(--space-2);
    height: var(--space-2);
    border-block-start: 2px solid var(--color-secondary);
    border-inline-end: 2px solid var(--color-secondary);
    background-color: transparent;
    transform: translate(-50%, 50%) rotate(135deg);
  }

  /* --- The block path ----------------------------------------------------
     The rail: three dashed strokes on one box, anchored to the `reviewed`
     card it hangs off. Stacked, it leaves reviewed's middle, runs up the
     gutter, and turns back in at coded's middle — so the box spans from one
     card's midline to the other's, which is `50%` either side of the gap.
     Dashed throughout, to read as a branch off the solid main flow rather
     than a fifth state. Aegean on the page base is 4.68:1, past the 3:1 a
     boundary that carried information would need — and this one does not
     carry it alone: the label names the path and the <li> it lives in names
     where the path starts. */
  .loop {
    position: absolute;
    inset-block-start: calc(-50% - var(--space-6));
    inset-block-end: 50%;
    inset-inline-end: 100%;
    width: var(--space-5);
    /* Overrides the global paragraph measure — this box is a rail, not a
       column of text. */
    max-width: none;
    border-block-start: 2px dashed var(--color-secondary);
    border-block-end: 2px dashed var(--color-secondary);
    border-inline-start: 2px dashed var(--color-secondary);
  }

  /* The arrowhead, centred on the rail's far corner — which sits exactly on
     coded's edge, so the path visibly re-enters the card rather than
     stopping in the gap. Same rotated-square chevron as the chain
     connectors: 45deg points it inward along the top stroke. */
  .loop::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    inset-block-start: 0;
    inset-inline-end: 0;
    width: var(--space-2);
    height: var(--space-2);
    border-block-start: 2px solid var(--color-secondary);
    border-inline-end: 2px solid var(--color-secondary);
    transform: translate(50%, -50%) rotate(45deg);
  }

  /* The label rides the rail at its midpoint — the middle of the gap between
     coded and reviewed — and carries the page background so the stroke it
     covers does not show through the chip. Muted text on base: 8.39:1 dark,
     5.66:1 light. nowrap keeps the chip one line high, which is what lets it
     sit inside the gap without touching either card. */
  .loop-label {
    position: absolute;
    inset-block-start: 50%;
    /* Back over the 2px stroke, so the chip covers the rail cleanly. */
    inset-inline-start: -2px;
    transform: translateY(-50%);
    width: max-content;
    padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-3);
    border: 1px dashed var(--color-secondary);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background-color: var(--color-bg);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--color-muted);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .closing {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-6);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  /* --- The row layout ----------------------------------------------------
     Every rule here overrides one above it, so the block has to come after
     all of them: same specificity means source order decides, and a media
     query adds none.

     1024px is a raw length by design: tokens.css owns colour, type and
     spacing and deliberately defines no breakpoint scale (the reasoning
     phase-1-ws-1.2 recorded for its 768px query). */
  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    .lifecycle {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
      gap: var(--space-5);
      /* The rail drops below the row here, so the stacked gutter closes. */
      padding-inline-start: var(--space-0);
    }

    /* The chain turns to run left-to-right: the line moves into the gap
       before each card, the chevron rotates to point along it. */
    .state:not(:first-child)::before {
      inset-block-end: auto;
      inset-block-start: 50%;
      inset-inline-start: auto;
      inset-inline-end: 100%;
      width: var(--space-5);
      height: 2px;
      transform: translateY(-50%);
    }

    .state:not(:first-child)::after {
      inset-block-end: auto;
      inset-block-start: 50%;
      inset-inline-start: auto;
      inset-inline-end: 100%;
      transform: translate(50%, -50%) rotate(45deg);
    }

    /* The rail turns with the flow: a U hanging below the row, from
       reviewed's centre back to coded's centre. All four tracks are equal,
       so one column plus one gap — `100% + --space-5` — is exactly the
       distance between two adjacent centres. */
    .loop {
      inset-block-start: 100%;
      inset-block-end: auto;
      height: var(--space-5);
      inset-inline-end: 50%;
      width: calc(100% + var(--space-5));
      border-block-start: none;
      border-inline-end: 2px dashed var(--color-secondary);
    }

    /* Now the far corner is the top of the left riser, on coded's bottom
       edge, so the head turns to point up into the card. */
    .loop::before {
      inset-inline-end: auto;
      inset-inline-start: 0;
      transform: translate(-50%, -50%) rotate(-45deg);
    }

    .loop-label {
      inset-block-start: auto;
      inset-block-end: 0;
      inset-inline-start: 50%;
      transform: translate(-50%, 50%);
    }

    /* Clears the rail and its label, which hang below the row without
       taking part in layout. */
    .closing {
      margin-block-start: var(--space-8);
    }
  }

  /* --- Staged reveal ------------------------------------------------------
     Opt-in only. Everything above renders the finished diagram, so no-JS,
     unsupported-observer and reduced-motion users all get the complete final
     state with no delay — the `is-revealed` class adds motion, it never adds
     visibility. `backwards` fill makes each state hold the start frame
     through its delay, so the stagger needs no hidden base state.
     320ms each, longest delay 560ms: inside ADR-0002's 200-700ms envelope,
     and nothing loops. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .is-revealed .state,
    .is-revealed .loop {
      animation: state-in 320ms var(--ease-out) backwards;
    }

    .is-revealed .state:nth-child(2) {
      animation-delay: 140ms;
    }

    .is-revealed .state:nth-child(3) {
      animation-delay: 280ms;
    }

    .is-revealed .state:nth-child(4) {
      animation-delay: 420ms;
    }

    .is-revealed .loop {
      animation-delay: 560ms;
    }
  }

  @keyframes state-in {
    from {
      opacity: 0;
      transform: translateY(var(--space-2));
    }
    to {
      opacity: 1;
      transform: none;
    }
  }

  /* The closing line lands on the same beat as the loop chip, the last piece
     of the diagram to arrive, so the section settles as one gesture rather
     than trailing a straggler. The h2 takes the page rule's 0ms default and
     needs no declaration. Delays are inert custom properties until that
     rule reads them, so no media query is needed here. */
  .closing {
    --reveal-delay: 560ms;
  }
</style>
